WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.280
Here are some common questions to ask when you&nbsp;
clean up data. Do variables have valid values?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.720
Are numeric variables within range? Are there&nbsp;
missing values? Are there duplicated values?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:27.520
Are values unique for some variables, for&nbsp;
example, ID variables? Are the dates valid?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:34.080
Do we need to combine multiple data files? It&nbsp;
is important to check those questions and handle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.440
them appropriately in the data cleanup process.&nbsp;
While it is straightforward to handle some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.520
those issues, dealing with others might not be&nbsp;
as simple. For example, handling missing values&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.960
usually requires good understanding of the problem&nbsp;
context and can take many alternative approaches.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:59.920
There are many commercial and open source tools&nbsp;
for data cleanup. Two very popular open source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.200
tools are OpenRefine and Data Wrangler.&nbsp;
The tools can greatly help many common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:11.840
data exploration tasks. You are encouraged&nbsp;
to explore their websites to learn more.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.800
Next, we move on to data transformation, which&nbsp;
usually means that we apply a mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.680
function to each data value. Perhaps&nbsp;
the most common data transformation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:29.040
centering and scaling of a single variable.&nbsp;
For those of you with statistics background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:35.200
you can think of it as calculating the z-score of&nbsp;
each observed value. That is, each data value is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:41.280
first reduced by the mean and then divided by the&nbsp;
standard deviation. One of the immediate benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.240
of centering and scaling is to make numerical&nbsp;
procedures easier to work with and more stable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.000
This is because the centering and scaling&nbsp;
ensures that multiple variables in a dataset is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.240
on a common scale. Centering and scaling is often&nbsp;
required or recommended for some modeling tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.080
such as clustering, principal component&nbsp;
analysis, and neural networks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.880
The main drawback is that the data becomes harder&nbsp;
to interpret. The data value after centering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.520
and scaling measures the number of standard&nbsp;
deviations between each data point and the mean.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:22.480
There are many other data transformations. Some&nbsp;
of them can be expressed using common mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:28.400
functions such logarithm, square, square&nbsp;
root, and inverse. Except for the first one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:34.320
all transformations mentioned here are considered&nbsp;
polynomial transformations. Because they involve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.920
polynomial terms of the original data value.&nbsp;
Different transformations are appropriate for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.920
different problem contexts. Sometimes, we have&nbsp;
to experiment to figure out the right one to use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.840
Each of those transformation can be easily&nbsp;
done in Excel and other data analysis tools.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.200
There are ways to automatically determine the&nbsp;
appropriate data transformation from the data.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:06.720
An example is Box-Cox transformation, which is&nbsp;
defined using a parameter called lambda. Different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.640
lambda values give different transformation.&nbsp;
When lambda is 0, the transformation is simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.560
a logarithm transformation. When lambda not&nbsp;
0, it is a polynomial transformation with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:25.120
parameter lambda. For example, when the lambda&nbsp;
= 2, it is effectively a square transformation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.480
Note here that offsetting by 1 and dividing&nbsp;
by lambda does not change the distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:36.480
of the variable. Similarly, when lambda equals&nbsp;
to a half, it is a square root transformation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.120
When lambda equals -1, it is&nbsp;
the inverse transformation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.320
Lambda can be estimated from the data&nbsp;
itself, and can take many different values.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.440
In this way, this transformation covers&nbsp;
all transformations discussed before.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.240
The transformations we discussed&nbsp;
so far work on a single variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.800
This should be contrasted with data&nbsp;
reduction, which reduces the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.080
by generating a smaller set of variables.&nbsp;
The purpose of data reduction is to use a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.840
smaller set of variables to capture most of&nbsp;
the information in the original variables.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.560
A widely used data reduction technique is&nbsp;
principal component analysis, which seeks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.440
find weighted averages of the variables to capture&nbsp;
most of information which is measured by variance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.400
in the data. The newly generated weighted&nbsp;
averages are known as principal components.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.920
The principal components are uncorrelated and&nbsp;
ideally a few of them are able to capture most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.640
of the variance in the data. An important&nbsp;
note here is that we need to first scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.600
the variables before applying principal&nbsp;
component analysis. This is so that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:55.840
principal components are not dominated by&nbsp;
variables that are much larger in scale.

